<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carignan-Salières Regiment</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1665">1665</a>–<a href="/years/1668">1668</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Carignan-Salières Regiment was a French infantry regiment sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a> in 1665, the first regular troops in the colony. Its roughly 1,200 men built forts along the Richelieu River and in 1666 burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> villages, forcing the Iroquois to make peace in 1667. More than 400 of its soldiers stayed on as settlers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carignan-salieres-regiment.jpg" alt="Carignan-Salières Regiment" width="320" height="447" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A. d'Auriac · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Regiment_de_Carignan-Salieres,_1665.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The regiment was formed in 1659 by merging the Carignan Regiment, raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> in 1644 by Thomas Francis of Savoy, Prince of Carignano, with the Salières Regiment, raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> early in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years War</a> by Johann von Balthazard. The Carignan had fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, Tuscany and Piedmont and for the royalists at the Battle of the Faubourg St Antoine in 1652. Henri de Chastelard de Salières commanded the merged unit.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>, with 3,035 people in 1660, could not hold off the Haudenosaunee raids of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaver-wars">Beaver Wars</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> made it a royal province in 1663, he and his finance minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-colbert">Jean-Baptiste Colbert</a> sent the regiment to end the threat and secure the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first ship left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rochelle">La Rochelle</a> on 19 April 1665, and the companies reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> between June and September, the last two transports with 35 dead on the voyage. The soldiers built Fort St. Louis, now Fort Chambly, Fort Saint-Jean and Fort Sainte Thérèse, restored Fort Richelieu and built Fort Sainte Anne on Isle La Motte. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> in the ranks were coerced into recanting.</p><p>Governor Daniel de Rémy de Courcelle's winter march of January 1666 went astray without its Algonquin guides, attacked cabins near Schenectady and turned back; the Jesuit Relations counted over 60 dead from hunger. A second strike under Pierre de Saurel turned back when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> returned their prisoners. In the autumn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquis-de-tracy">Alexandre de Prouville de Tracy</a> led 600 soldiers, 600 Canadien volunteers and 100 Algonquin and Wendat warriors to the four Mohawk villages, found them abandoned, claimed them for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> on 17 October 1666 and burned them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Soldiers who stayed were offered money or rations, officers seigneuries along the Richelieu, placed as a buffer against the Haudenosaunee and the English. Many married Filles du Roi, and their soldiers' nicknames became family surnames and place names. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> the regiment fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-dutch-war">Franco-Dutch War</a>, became the Soissons Regiment in 1676 and the Perche Regiment in 1690, and its line ran on to the 24th and 50th Infantry of the Line.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Carignan-Salières Regiment” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1665 Louis XIV sent the <b>Carignan-Salières Regiment</b>, some 1,200 men, which burned Mohawk villages in 1666 and forced a peace.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Military</a></li></ul></section>
